## Measurement Protocol v2: Beyond the Browser

Measurement Protocol v2 is GA4’s REST API for sending events from any server, device, or system. It’s essential for:
- Offline conversions
- Server-to-server tracking
- CRM integrations
- IoT device telemetry
- Backend order processing

### Core Endpoints

| Endpoint | Purpose |
|--------|--------|
| `https://www.google-analytics.com/mp/collect` | Send events to GA4 |
| `https://www.google-analytics.com/debug/mp/collect` | Validate payloads (debug mode) |
| `https://www.google-analytics.com/mp/debug/collect` | Same as above (older path) |

### Building a Valid MPv2 Payload

```json
{
  "client_id": "12345.67890",
  "events": [{
    "name": "purchase",
    "params": {
      "transaction_id": "T12345",
      "affiliation": "Online Store",
      "value": 99.99,
      "currency": "USD",
      "tax": 7.99,
      "shipping": 5.99,
      "items": [
        {
          "item_id": "SKU123",
          "item_name": "Blue T-Shirt",
          "quantity": 1,
          "price": 19.99
        }
      ]
    }
  }]
}
```

> **Note**: Items must use the `items` array with `item_id`, `item_name`, and `quantity`. Price is optional if included in parent `value`.

### Best Practices for MPv2

- **Use POST, not GET**: GET requests are limited to ~2000 characters and not recommended.
- **Set `timestamp_micros`**: Preserve event time for offline events.
- **Include `user_id`**: When known, to stitch sessions across devices.
- **Use `non_personalized_ads: true`**: If collecting data from logged-out users in regions with consent requirements.
- **Batch events**: Send up to 25 events per request to reduce overhead.

```bash
curl -X POST "https://www.google-analytics.com/mp/collect?measurement_id=G-XXXXXX&api_secret=YOUR_SECRET" \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d '{
    "client_id": "123.456",
    "events": [{
      "name": "signup",
      "params": {
        "method": "email",
        "user_id": "U789",
        "timestamp_micros": 1700000000000000
      }
    }]
  }'
```

> **Pitfall**: Forgetting `api_secret` or `measurement_id` in the query string results in silent failures. Always log request URLs for debugging.

### Integrating with CRM Systems

Most CRMs (Salesforce, HubSpot, etc.) support webhooks or REST APIs. Use a middleware service to transform CRM data into GA4-compatible events.

Example: HubSpot deal closed webhook → GA4 purchase event.

```javascript
app.post('/hubspot/webhook', (req, res) => {
  const deal = req.body.object.properties;
  const payload = {
    client_id: deal.hs_analytics_user_id,  // May need mapping
    events: [{
      name: 'purchase',
      params: {
        transaction_id: deal.dealname,
        affiliation: 'HubSpot CRM',
        value: parseFloat(deal.amount),
        currency: deal.currency || 'USD',
        items: [{
          item_id: deal.product_id,
          item_name: deal.product_name,
          quantity: 1,
          price: parseFloat(deal.amount)
        }]
      }
    }]
  };

  // Send via MPv2
  fetchMP(payload);
  res.status(200).send('OK');
});
```

> **Data Alignment Tip**: Use a lookup table to map CRM field names (`dealname`, `hs_analytics_user_id`) to GA4 parameter names (`transaction_id`, `client_id`). This prevents semantic drift.

